## 3.2 Robot Control

robotctl is an NSH builtin that uses the Stage 2 /dev/pwm0 resource only
inside an explicit actuator command. help and status never open or start PWM.
Boot always reaches NSH with PWM stopped.

The five PWM lower-half entries are fixed by the board defconfig:

| pwm_info_s.channels[] index | NuttX channel ID | GPIO | Servo |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | 1 | GPIO9 | RF |
| 1 | 2 | GPIO10 | RR |
| 2 | 3 | GPIO21 | LR |
| 3 | 4 | GPIO47 | LF |
| 4 | 5 | GPIO48 | TAIL |

Servo output is 50 Hz. robotctl clamps logical angles to 0..180 and converts
them to 500..2500 us pulses through the NuttX PWM ub16_t duty representation.
The current trim constants are all zero and require mechanical calibration
before normal use.

Available commands:

~~~text
robotctl help
robotctl status
robotctl home
robotctl off
robotctl servo <lf|lr|rf|rr|tail> <angle>
robotctl servo-off
robotctl forward [steps] [period_ms]
robotctl backward [steps] [period_ms]
robotctl left [steps] [period_ms]
robotctl right [steps] [period_ms]
~~~

The default motion mode is POWER_SAFE. It changes one servo at a time, uses
three-degree steps with a 25-30 ms interval, programs zero duty between
substeps, and stops PWM after the command. HOME uses LF, RF, LR, RR, then
TAIL, with 30 ms steps and a 200 ms settle. Gaits retain the reference target
angles but split every two-servo phase into single-servo substeps. The actual
duration can exceed period_ms; brownout avoidance takes priority.

robotctl off and servo-off first set all five duties to zero, then issue
PWMIOC_STOP and close /dev/pwm0. OFF disables PWM pulses; it does not make a
gear-reduced SG90/MG90 mechanically free-spinning. Verify HOME/OFF with a
logic analyzer, oscilloscope, or servo 5 V bus current rather than hand force.
The ESP32-S3 LEDC lower-half has no per-channel disable operation, so inactive
channels remain routed but receive zero duty, not a valid servo control pulse.

For the first hardware test, suspend the robot so the legs cannot contact a
surface and use only:

~~~sh
robotctl help
robotctl status
robotctl home
robotctl off
~~~

